var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-set"]=(()=>{var n=Object.defineProperty;var s=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(t,e)=>{for(var a in e)n(t,a,{get:e[a],enumerable:!0})},u=(t,e,a,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let o of h(e))!d.call(t,o)&&o!==a&&n(t,o,{get:()=>e[o],enumerable:!(i=s(e,o))||i.enumerable});return t};var f=t=>u(n({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),t);var y={};l(y,{journey:()=>r});var r={id:"deity-set",kind:"deity",paths:["egyptian"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/set",title:"Set: Great of Strength",summary:"A month with Sutekh, lord of storms, the desert and foreign lands: murderer of Osiris, rival of Horus, and defender of Ra\u2019s boat against the serpent of chaos. Study his whole history, from honoured god of kings to later outcast, keep ten nights at the edge, and decide whether to dedicate.",welcome:`This journey spends about a lunar month with Set, the Egyptian god of storms, the desert, foreign lands and raw strength. He is one of the most misunderstood gods of Egypt. He killed his brother Osiris and fought Horus for the throne, and in the last centuries of Egyptian religion he was cast out as an enemy. But for most of that history he was also honoured: a god of kings, a protector of the sun god, a patron of soldiers and of the oases, whose strength was needed at the edges of the ordered world.

He suits people who know what anger, force and wildness feel like from the inside and want to put them to good use, people who live or work at the edges, and anyone who wants to understand that a god can be dangerous and necessary at once. You will learn his story from the Egyptian sources, the long contest with Horus, his place in the prow of Ra's boat, his worship under the Ramesside kings, and how and why his reputation fell. For ten nights you will keep a rite at the edge of your home. At the end you may dedicate yourself to him, or not.

This is a self-dedication. It is not an initiation into Kemetic Orthodoxy, the Temple of Set or any other group, and it does not replace one. It is also not a path of cursing, harm or "dark" glamour. Set's strength, as Egyptians honoured it, was turned against chaos, not against people.`,about:`Set (also Seth, Egyptian Sutekh or Suty) was a son of Geb and Nut, brother of Osiris, Isis and Nephthys, and husband of Nephthys. He was lord of the red land, the desert that surrounds the black soil of the Nile valley, of storms and thunder, of foreign countries and of great strength; his title Aa-pehty means "great of strength". He is shown with the head, or the whole body, of a creature no one has identified: curved snout, tall squared ears and a stiff forked tail. In myth he murdered Osiris and fought a long contest with Horus for the throne, yet every night he stood in the prow of Ra's boat and speared the serpent Apep, the chaos that tried to swallow the sun. His oldest city was Nubt (Ombos, near modern Naqada); later he was the great god of Avaris in the Delta and of the oases of the Western Desert. Kings of the Second Dynasty set his animal over their names, and the Ramesside kings of the New Kingdom, among them Seti I, "the one of Set", took him as their patron. Only in the first millennium BCE was he widely treated as an enemy, his images defaced and his name avoided, though he was still worshipped in the oases for centuries. Today some Kemetics honour him, often as the defender of Ra's boat, while others keep him at a distance; the Temple of Set, founded in 1975, is a modern esoteric movement with its own ideas.`,notes:['Set\u2019s reputation changed over three thousand years. Early and New Kingdom sources honour him; late sources, Greek writers and the Edfu temple texts often treat him as an enemy. Neither picture is the "real" Set; both are his history.',"Set is not Satan. The comparison is modern and was popularised in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries; there is no ancient link between them.","The Temple of Set, founded by Michael Aquino in 1975, is a modern Left-Hand Path organisation that grew out of the Church of Satan. It is a real religious body with its own teachings, distinct from ancient Egyptian worship and from Kemetic practice. Be clear which you are approaching.","Kemetic communities differ on Set. Some honour him openly, some only as the defender of Ra\u2019s boat, and some not at all, especially at times of Osiris\u2019s mourning. Be courteous in their spaces and follow their customs.","This journey does not include curses, binding or harming others. If you are carrying anger that frightens you, or are in a violent situation, please seek help from people and professionals; in danger, call your local emergency number.","Safety: stay indoors in thunderstorms and never go into deserts or wild places unprepared. Keep red wine and beer away from pets and from anyone who should not drink."],holyDays:[],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"The Red Land",intro:"Begin by learning who Set is and making him a shrine at the edge of your home. The threshold ends with a short introduction rite: you greet him by his titles and give him water, bread and incense.",days:3,lessons:[{id:"set-who",title:"Sutekh, Great of Strength",summary:"His name, his strange animal, his place in the family of the gods, and the red land he ruled.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The animal no one can name",body:`Set is the only great Egyptian god whose animal no one can identify. He is shown with a long, down-curved snout, tall ears squared off at the top and, when shown as a whole animal, a stiff upright tail split at the end like an arrow's fletching. Scholars have suggested the aardvark, the okapi, the jackal, the donkey, the pig and the greyhound, and none fits. Most Egyptologists now think it was a fabulous animal, or one stylised so far from life that it no longer matters. Egyptologists simply call it the Set animal.

That seems right for him. Set is the god of what does not fit: the desert beyond the fields, the foreign land beyond the border, the storm that comes from nowhere. Other animals were linked with him too, among them the donkey, the oryx, the pig, the hippopotamus and the crocodile, mostly in later texts that saw him as hostile.`},{heading:"His names",body:`The Egyptian name is usually written Set, Seth or Sutekh; Suty appears in some New Kingdom names. Its meaning is unknown. His great title was Aa-pehty, "great of strength", and he was Nubty, "he of Nubt", after his old city. He was lord of Upper Egypt in some texts, lord of the desert and of foreign lands, and lord of the oasis.

Kemetic practitioners variously say Set, Sutekh or Seth. Any is respectful. Many people find that "great of strength" is the title that opens their prayers.`},{heading:"The red land",body:`Egypt was two lands of colour. Kemet, the black land, was the Nile valley, soaked each year by the flood and black with silt. Deshret, the red land, was the desert on either side: dry, empty, beautiful and deadly, where caravans crossed, metals and stones were mined, and the dead were buried at its edge. Set was the lord of Deshret.

Red was his colour. That made him the god of heat, dryness, rage and blood, but also of the gold and copper that came from the desert, and of the strength it took to survive there. Plutarch, writing for Greeks around 100 CE, says that Egyptians disliked red-haired people and red animals because of Set, and that some sacrificed red oxen to him. The desert was not evil in Egyptian thought. It was the outside: necessary, dangerous and full of power.`},{heading:"His family",body:`In the theology of Heliopolis, Set was one of the four children of Geb and Nut, born on the third of the five days upon the year. Plutarch says he was born not at the right time or place but burst from his mother's side. He married his sister Nephthys, and the couple had no children together in most sources; in Plutarch's later telling, Nephthys bore Anubis to Osiris instead.

Set's wives also included foreign goddesses, Anat and Astarte, whom Egyptians took over from Canaan in the New Kingdom; the Contendings of Horus and Set suggests giving them to him in compensation for the throne. This is fitting for a lord of foreign lands. In the New Kingdom he was also identified with the Canaanite storm god Baal, especially at Avaris, the Delta city where he was greatest.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Set is attested from the earliest dynastic period, with his animal on royal monuments of the Second Dynasty; his titles, his place among the children of Geb and Nut, his marriage to Nephthys and his link with foreign goddesses and Baal are well documented.",unknown:"The meaning of his name and the identity of his animal are unknown. How Egyptians of different periods felt about him day to day, as opposed to what kings and priests wrote, is hard to recover."},inPractice:'Choose a name (Set, Sutekh or Seth) and an image: the Set animal standing, or a man with its head holding the was sceptre. Learn "great of strength" (Aa-pehty) by heart. Decide where his shrine will go: many devotees place it near an outside door or window, at the edge of the home, as the desert lay at the edge of the land.',reflect:["What in you does not fit, and has been treated as a problem? What strength might be in it?","What do you feel when you think of Set: unease, kinship, curiosity, anger? Write it down honestly."],further:["Herman te Velde, Seth, God of Confusion: A Study of His Role in Egyptian Mythology and Religion (1967; 2nd edn 1977)","Richard H. Wilkinson, The Complete Gods and Goddesses of Ancient Egypt (2003), entry on Seth"],library:["traditional/deity/set","traditional/deity/nephthys","traditional/deity/osiris","traditional/crystal/red_jasper"]}],practices:[{id:"set-shrine",title:"Set up a shrine at the edge",instructions:`Choose a shelf or small table near an outside door or window, or in a part of the home that feels like an edge. Clean it with water that has a pinch of natron, washing soda or salt dissolved in it.

Lay a clean red, ochre or black cloth. Set his image at the back. In front, place a bowl for water, a dish for bread, an incense burner on a heatproof tile, and a red or black candle. Add, if you like, a small dish of clean sand, a piece of red jasper or hematite, a picture of a desert or a storm, or an iron nail; Plutarch says Egyptians called iron "the bone of Typhon", their name for Set.

Keep the shrine tidy and plain. Cover the image with a cloth between visits if you can, as Egyptian shrines were closed when the god was not being served.`,times:1,prompt:"Where did you place his shrine, and how did it feel to make room for him?"}],rite:{title:"Introduction to Set",summary:"A short first visit: purify, open the shrine, greet him by his titles, offer water, bread and incense, and tell him plainly why you have come.",minutes:20,ingredients:[{name:"Set",ref:"traditional/deity/set"},{name:"An offering bowl of fresh, cool water",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"A pinch of natron, washing soda or salt in a cup of water, for washing"},{name:"A piece of bread",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"Frankincense, myrrh or cedar incense",ref:"traditional/herb/cedar"},{name:"An incense burner on a heatproof tile",ref:"traditional/tool/incense_burner"},{name:"A red or black candle",ref:"traditional/candle/red"}],preparation:"Wash your hands and face and rinse your mouth with the natron or salt water, spitting it out. Wear clean clothes. Have the shrine clean and covered. Turn off your phone.",steps:[{title:"Light and purify",instructions:"Light the candle and the incense. Touch the natron water to your lips and pass your hands through the smoke.",light:!0,spoken:"I am pure. My hands are washed and my mouth is clean. I come before the lord of the red land."},{title:"Open the shrine",instructions:"Uncover his image.",spoken:"Your face is uncovered, Sutekh. Awake in peace, great of strength."},{title:"Adore",instructions:"Raise both hands, palms toward him, at the height of your face, and speak.",spoken:"Homage to you, Set, great of strength, lord of the red land and the storm, you who stand in the prow of the boat of Ra and spear the serpent: I greet you."},{title:"Offer",instructions:"Lift the water, then the bread, toward him and set them down. Hold the incense up for a moment.",spoken:"An offering for Set: cool water, bread and sweet smoke. I give them freely. May they please you."},{title:"Say why you have come",instructions:"Speak plainly and honestly, in your own words: your name, what drew you to him, what you are wary of, and that you will spend a month learning before you decide anything. Then sit quietly.",minutes:5},{title:"Close and withdraw",instructions:"Thank him. Cover the shrine and snuff the candle. Step back before you turn away.",snuff:!0,spoken:"Go in peace, Set. I will come again."}],closing:"Eat the bread later or the next morning; that is the reversion of offerings. Pour the water on the earth outside. Write a few honest lines in your journal.",safety:"Keep candle and incense on a heatproof surface in a ventilated room, away from curtains, children, pets and pet birds, and never leave them burning unattended. Do not swallow the natron water."}},{kind:"study",title:"Murderer, Rival and Defender",intro:"Three lessons: Set in the myth of Osiris and Horus; Set in the prow of Ra\u2019s boat; and the long history of his worship and its fall. Keep a short visit to his shrine in between.",days:11,lessons:[{id:"set-myth",title:"The Murder and the Contest",summary:"Set\u2019s part in the death of Osiris and the long quarrel with Horus, from the Egyptian sources and from Plutarch.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The death of Osiris",body:`The Pyramid Texts, carved in royal burial chambers from about 2350 BCE, already know that Set killed his brother Osiris. They say it briefly and indirectly, as Egyptian texts usually do about Osiris's death: Set struck him down on the riverbank at a place called Nedyt, and Osiris lay there until Isis and Nephthys found him. Egyptians preferred not to describe the murder of a god in detail. Spoken words had power, and to tell it fully was to make it happen again.

Plutarch, writing in Greek around 100 CE, gives the story everyone knows: Set, whom he calls by the Greek name Typhon, tricks Osiris into a chest at a banquet, seals it and throws it into the Nile, and later tears the body into fourteen pieces. Some Egyptian sources do speak of the body being divided, but the chest and the banquet come only from him. When you tell the story, say whose version you are telling.`},{heading:"The contest with Horus",body:`When Horus, the son of Osiris and Isis, grew up, he claimed his father's throne, and Set refused to give it up. Their struggle is one of the oldest myths in Egypt. In the Pyramid Texts each wounds the other: Set tears out Horus's eye, Horus injures Set's testicles. Neither comes out whole.

The fullest telling is the Contendings of Horus and Set, a New Kingdom story in Papyrus Chester Beatty I, from about 1150 BCE. For eighty years a divine court argues. Ra, the sun god, favours Set, the experienced and powerful elder; others favour Horus, the rightful son. Set proposes contests: a fight as hippopotamuses under water, a race in stone boats. There is a crude episode in which Set tries to shame Horus sexually, and Isis turns the trick back on him so that Set is shamed instead, through a lettuce he eats; lettuce was Set's favourite food and a sign of virility. The story is coarse, comic and political, and it treats Set as a strong, blunt, tricked and sometimes sympathetic figure rather than a monster.`},{heading:"How it ended",body:`The court finally gives the throne to Horus. But Set is not destroyed. In the Contendings, Ra says: let Set be given to me, to live with me as a son, and he shall thunder in the sky and be feared. So Set goes up to the sun.

Other sources tell the end differently. In the Memphite Theology, a text carved on the Shabaka Stone around 700 BCE from an older original, the earth god Geb first divides Egypt between them, Upper Egypt to Set and Lower Egypt to Horus, and then gives the whole to Horus. On royal throne bases, Horus and Set together tie the plants of the two lands around the sign for "union". In Egyptian thinking order did not mean wiping out its opposite. It meant each power in its place.`},{heading:"What the myth asks",body:`Set did a terrible thing. Killing a brother was a crime in Egypt as anywhere, and devotees of Set do not need to excuse it. But Egyptian religion did not make him simply the devil of the story. He was a god with a function: force, strength, disruption and the wild. When that force serves order it defends the world; when it turns against order it destroys.

That is why many modern devotees find Set useful. Most people carry some anger, restlessness, ambition or wildness. The myth does not say "destroy it". It says: give it its proper place, and point it at the real enemy.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The murder of Osiris and the contest with Horus are attested from the Pyramid Texts; the Contendings in Papyrus Chester Beatty I; the division of Egypt in the Memphite Theology on the Shabaka Stone (British Museum EA 498); the binding of the two lands by Horus and Set on royal monuments.",unknown:"There was no single official version, and Egyptian texts avoid describing the murder. The chest and banquet are Plutarch\u2019s. Older theories that the myth records real wars between followers of Horus and Set are now widely doubted, though some scholars still argue for a political background."},inPractice:"Read the Contendings of Horus and Set in a modern translation and notice how Set is drawn: not as evil, but as strong, stubborn and outplayed. Write down one thing Set does in the story that you recognise in yourself, honestly. At his shrine, name it, and ask for the strength to turn it to good use.",reflect:["Where has your own strength or anger done harm, and where has it protected?","How do you feel about honouring a god who killed his brother? Write it down without tidying it up."],further:["Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature, vols. 1 and 2 (1973, 1976), for the Memphite Theology and the Contendings","Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris, trans. F. C. Babbitt (Loeb, 1936; public domain, at LacusCurtius)","J. Gwyn Griffiths, The Conflict of Horus and Seth from Egyptian and Classical Sources (1960)"],library:["traditional/deity/horus","traditional/deity/osiris","traditional/deity/isis"]},{id:"set-barque",title:"In the Prow of Ra\u2019s Boat",summary:"Set as the defender of the sun against Apep, and the strength that order needs.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The night journey",body:`Every evening, Egyptians believed, the sun god Ra entered the underworld in his night boat and sailed through twelve hours of darkness before being reborn at dawn. The danger on that journey was Apep (Greek Apophis), a vast serpent of chaos who tried to swallow the sun or drink the water from under the boat. Apep was not a god to honour. He was the opposite of creation, and he could never be destroyed for good, only defeated again each night.

The boat had a crew. Thoth, Ma'at, Hu and Sia sailed with Ra, and Horus held the steering oar. And standing in the prow, spear in hand, was Set.`},{heading:"Set against Apep",body:`Several texts and images show Set spearing Apep from the bow of the sun boat. In a spell found in the Coffin Texts and later in the Book of the Dead (chapter 108), Set drives the serpent back with his spear. A well-known vignette in a Twenty-first Dynasty funerary papyrus, the Papyrus of Herweben in the Egyptian Museum, shows the Set-headed god leaning from the prow to drive his spear into the coiling snake. In the Contendings, it is because Set's strength is needed in the sun boat that Ra takes him into the sky.

So the same strength that killed Osiris is the strength that keeps the sun alive. The wildest of the gods is the one you want at the front of the boat.`},{heading:"Strength that serves order",body:`This is the centre of Set's positive religion. Egyptian order, ma'at, was not gentle or tidy. It had to be defended every day, and defending it needed force: soldiers at the borders, storms that cleared the air, a protector who did not flinch. Kings boasted of having the strength of Set in battle. New Kingdom reliefs show Set, with Horus, teaching the king to shoot the bow.

For a modern devotee, this is where his practice lives. Set is honoured not for chaos itself but for strength aimed at chaos: courage in a crisis, physical effort, endurance, saying no, protecting what is fragile from what would swallow it.`},{heading:"Ambiguity is the point",body:`The Dutch Egyptologist Herman te Velde, whose 1967 book is still the standard study, called Set the "god of confusion": a god of boundaries crossed, of disorder and of things out of place. But te Velde also showed that Egyptians needed him. Without Set's disorder there is no struggle and no renewal; without his strength the sun is swallowed.

You do not have to resolve Set into a good god or a bad one. Egyptians did not. You can hold both, the murderer and the defender, as one god whose power is real and must be pointed in the right direction. That is the practice: knowing your own force, and choosing where it goes.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Set as defender of the sun boat appears in Coffin Texts, in New Kingdom sources and in Third Intermediate Period funerary papyri; Apep and the night journey are well attested in the Amduat, Book of Gates and the Book of Overthrowing Apep. Kings\u2019 claims to Set\u2019s strength appear in battle texts.",unknown:"How widely Set\u2019s role in the sun boat was known, compared with Horus or other defenders, varied by period. In many texts other gods fight Apep instead. Whether ordinary Egyptians prayed to Set for protection at home is little attested."},inPractice:"Find a photograph of Set spearing Apep and keep it on his shrine. Each evening this week, at sunset, give a short word of support for the sun boat\u2019s safe passage, as some Kemetics do. Keep it about chaos and protection, never about people. Offer him one act of physical effort or courage.",reflect:["What is your Apep, the chaos you must face down again and again?","Where could your strength be most useful right now, and to whom?"],further:["Herman te Velde, Seth, God of Confusion (1967; 2nd edn 1977)","Erik Hornung, The Ancient Egyptian Books of the Afterlife (1999)","R. O. Faulkner, The Ancient Egyptian Coffin Texts (1973\u201378)"],library:["traditional/deity/ra","traditional/deity/set","traditional/crystal/hematite","traditional/crystal/obsidian"]},{id:"set-history",title:"From the God of Kings to the Outcast",summary:"How Set was honoured at Nubt, Avaris and in the oases, chosen by the Ramesside kings, and later defaced and cast out.",minutes:9,sections:[{heading:"Nubt and the first kings",body:`Set's oldest city was Nubt, "gold town", near modern Naqada in Upper Egypt, close to the gold routes into the Eastern Desert. The Greeks called it Ombos. His cult there goes back before the First Dynasty. Late in the Second Dynasty, around 2700 BCE, a king named Peribsen put the Set animal, rather than the Horus falcon, on top of his name, and his successor Khasekhemwy put both, Horus and Set together. Whatever the politics behind that, it shows that Set could stand for kingship itself.

In the Pyramid Texts Set is both the murderer of Osiris and a power the dead king needs. Together with Horus he helps the king up the ladder to the sky, and the king claims the strength of Set as his own.`},{heading:"Avaris and the Ramesside kings",body:`In the Second Intermediate Period, around 1650 to 1550 BCE, kings of foreign origin, the Hyksos, ruled the north from Avaris in the eastern Delta. They worshipped Set there, identified with the storm god Baal. When the Hyksos were driven out, the Theban victors did not abolish him. Avaris remained his city.

In the New Kingdom a family from that region came to the throne: the Ramessides. Their founder Ramesses I was an army officer from the Delta, and his son was named Seti, "the one of Set". Seti I, one of Egypt's great builders, and his son Ramesses II honoured Set openly. Ramesses II set up a stela, now called the Four Hundred Year Stela, celebrating four hundred years of Set's cult at Avaris. One of the four divisions of his army was named after Set. A later king, Setnakhte, founded the Twentieth Dynasty with Set's name in his own. For these kings, Set was a patron of strength, the army and the north.`},{heading:"The fall",body:`In the first millennium BCE, Set's reputation fell sharply. Egypt was ruled more and more by foreign powers, Libyans, Kushites, Assyrians, Persians, and Set, lord of foreign lands, came to stand for the invader and the enemy. Osiris's cult grew ever more central, and Set was his murderer. In many places his images were defaced and his name chiselled out or replaced with that of another god; on some statues his animal head was recut. In the Ptolemaic temple texts at Edfu, he is the hippopotamus whom Horus harpoons in a sacred drama, and in rituals his image was destroyed.

Greek writers matched him with Typhon, the monster who fought Zeus, and Plutarch makes him the irrational, violent part of the world and the soul. That is the Set most people met in books for two thousand years.`},{heading:"The god of the oases",body:`But Set was never wholly cast out. In the oases of the Western Desert, especially Dakhla, he remained a chief god into the first millennium BCE as "lord of the oasis". Excavations at Mut el-Kharab, the ancient capital of Dakhla, have uncovered his temple, and a Twenty-second Dynasty stela, the Dakhla Stela, records a water dispute settled in his name. Out in the red land, where he had always belonged, he went on being honoured as a protector and a giver of water and life.

So his history is not a single story of good or bad. He was feared and honoured, blamed and needed, demonised and kept, depending on who was writing, when and where. To know Set well is to hold that whole history.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Peribsen\u2019s and Khasekhemwy\u2019s names, Set\u2019s cult at Nubt and Avaris, the Four Hundred Year Stela (found at Tanis), the names of Seti I and Setnakhte, the army division of Set, the defacement of Set images in later periods, the Edfu Triumph of Horus, and the temple and Dakhla Stela at Mut el-Kharab are all documented.",unknown:"Why Peribsen chose Set is debated: religion, politics, civil war or something else. When and how fast Set\u2019s demonisation spread varied by region, and the role of foreign rule in it is an interpretation, though a widely held one. How long his worship lasted in the oases is not certain."},inPractice:"Look up photographs of the Ramesside kings\u2019 monuments and of a defaced Set figure. Keep both in mind at his shrine: the god honoured and the god scratched out. If you write prayers to him, draw on the Ramesside titles: great of strength, lord of the north, beloved of the king.",reflect:["How does it change your sense of Set to know he was honoured by Egypt\u2019s greatest builders?","Have you ever been the one blamed for everything, or done the blaming? What does Set\u2019s history say to that?"],further:["Herman te Velde, Seth, God of Confusion (1967; 2nd edn 1977)",'Olaf E. Kaper, "The Statue of Penbast: On the Cult of Seth in the Dakhleh Oasis", in Essays on Ancient Egypt in Honour of Herman te Velde (1997)',"Toby Wilkinson, Early Dynastic Egypt (1999), on Peribsen and Khasekhemwy"],library:["traditional/deity/set","traditional/deity/horus","traditional/deity/ra"]}],practices:[{id:"set-evening-water",title:"Water at the edge",instructions:"Each evening, wash your hands, uncover his shrine, pour fresh water into his bowl, raise your hands and say one of his titles. Then name one hard thing you did or faced that day. Pour yesterday\u2019s water on the earth outside. Cover the shrine.",times:11,every:"day",prompt:"One line: what took strength today?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Ten Nights at the Edge",intro:"For ten nights, one Egyptian ten-day week, you keep a short rite at his shrine with an offering and a theme. Several themes ask for an act, not only words. Read the lesson on honouring him today before you begin.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"set-today",title:"Set Today: Practice, Days and Care",summary:"Who honours Set now and how, what to offer, his days, and the sensitive points.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"Who honours him now",body:`Within Kemetic religion, the modern revival of Egyptian religion, Set is a real point of difference. Kemetic Orthodoxy, the temple founded in 1988 by Tamara Siuda, honours Set as one of the gods and has members who are devoted to him; it follows its own customs, including about when he is honoured. Many reconstructionists honour him as the defender of Ra's boat, sometimes keeping his shrine apart from that of Osiris. Others do not honour him at all, following the later Egyptian view. All of these have support in the ancient record.

Outside Kemetic religion, the Temple of Set, founded in 1975 by Michael Aquino after he left the Church of Satan, honours Set as a figure of individual will and self-deification within a modern Left-Hand Path philosophy. It is a sincere modern religion with its own literature. It is not ancient Egyptian worship, and this journey does not follow it. Some eclectic Pagans also honour Set as a god of storms, strength and the outsider.`},{heading:"What to offer and how",body:`Bread, beer and water are the offerings of every Egyptian god, and suit him. Red offerings are traditional in the sense that red was his colour: red wine or red beer, red juice for those who do not drink, red fruit. Incense of frankincense, myrrh or cedar. Plutarch's note that some Egyptians offered red oxen to Set has led some modern devotees to offer red meat, cooked and then eaten in the reversion of offerings; that is your choice.

Effort is an offering in his spirit: a long walk in bad weather, a hard workout, a difficult conversation you have been avoiding, standing between someone vulnerable and a threat. Offer it by name at his shrine. Keep the Egyptian shape (purify, open, adore, offer, sit, close) and eat or share the food afterwards.`},{heading:"His days",body:`Set's birthday is the third of the five epagomenal days, the days upon the year before Wep Ronpet, the new year. Egyptian calendars of lucky and unlucky days, such as the Cairo Calendar, counted that day unlucky, and advised against starting anything important on it. Many Kemetics honour him quietly on it, with a simple offering, and do no other ritual business. Storm days are a natural time to greet him from a safe place indoors.

Some Kemetics keep apart from Set during the Khoiak mysteries of Osiris, out of respect for Osiris's mourning. Others mark his place in the sun boat at sunset all year. Choose a calendar and an approach and keep them consistently.`},{heading:"Sensitive points",body:`Set attracts people for good reasons and for difficult ones. If you are drawn to him because you want permission for cruelty, or power over others, or a dark image to wear, pause. Egyptian sources honour Set's strength when it serves order and protection; they never honour harm to the innocent. This journey contains no curses and no binding, and that is deliberate.

Anger is real and can be useful, but it can also hurt you and people around you. If your anger frightens you, or you are in a violent relationship on either side, please talk to someone you trust or a professional, and in danger call your local emergency number. Be courteous in Kemetic spaces where Set is not honoured. And do not call him Satan, or let others do so unchallenged; it is a modern slander, not his history.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Kemetic Orthodoxy and the Temple of Set are documented modern religious bodies. The epagomenal birthdays and the unlucky third day in calendars such as Papyrus Cairo 86637 are attested. Red as Set\u2019s colour, and Plutarch\u2019s remarks on red animals, are recorded.",unknown:"There is no single modern Kemetic view of Set, and no agreed calendar. Whether ordinary ancient Egyptians made offerings to Set at home, and what they offered, is little known. Plutarch\u2019s red ox sacrifices may reflect hostile rites against Set as much as worship of him."},inPractice:"Fix your offering habit: water most evenings, a red offering and bread weekly, and one offered effort each week. Mark his birthday quietly. Decide how you will handle Khoiak and Osiris\u2019s mourning if you also honour Osiris, and be consistent.",reflect:["Why are you drawn to Set? Write the honest answer, even if it is uncomfortable.","What would it mean to offer your strength rather than only your words?"],further:["Richard J. Reidy, Eternal Egypt: Ancient Rituals for the Modern World (2010)","Herman te Velde, Seth, God of Confusion (1967; 2nd edn 1977)"],library:["traditional/deity/set","traditional/crystal/smoky_quartz","traditional/candle/black","traditional/herb/frankincense"],premade:["folk-build-your-own-ritual"]}],practices:[{id:"set-ten-nights",title:"Ten nights at the edge",instructions:`Each night: wash, uncover the shrine, light the candle and incense, raise your hands and greet him as great of strength. Give water and bread, and on some nights a little red wine or juice. Keep the night's theme for five to ten minutes, then journal. Thank him, cover the shrine and snuff the flame. Eat or share the bread tomorrow.

Night 1: the red land. Picture the desert beyond the fields. Night 2: the animal no one can name; what in you does not fit? Night 3: the brother he killed; sit with harm honestly, yours or done to you. Night 4: the long contest; a struggle you are in. Night 5: the prow of the boat; name your Apep. Night 6: an act of strength; do something physically hard today and offer it. Night 7: the kings who loved him; a time your strength protected someone. Night 8: the defaced god; a time you were blamed or blamed another. Night 9: the god of the oasis; where you give water in a dry place. Night 10: what you would ask of him, and what you would offer back.

If you miss a night, carry on where you left off. A battery candle is a fine alternative to a flame.`,times:10,every:"day",prompt:"What did tonight\u2019s theme stir? What did you notice in yourself?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"Weighing the Storm",intro:"Take a few days with only the evening water. Read your journal from the beginning. Discernment is not a contest you win; deciding to wait, to stop, or to honour another god instead is a good and honest outcome.",days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"set-reread",title:"Read the month back",instructions:"Read your journal from the first entry in one sitting. Mark the places where your practice made you stronger and steadier, and the places where it fed something you are not proud of. Then answer the questions below.",times:1,prompt:"Has this month made your strength more useful to others, or less?"}],questions:["Are you drawn to Set himself, the god of strength who defends the sun, or mainly to an image of darkness or rebellion? Both are worth knowing about yourself.","Can you honour Set without excusing harm, including harm you have done? What would keep that honest?","If you also honour Osiris, Isis or Horus, how will you hold Set beside them? Can you live with that tension?","Would a Kemetic community that honours Set, with its own teachers and customs, serve you better than a self-dedication?","If you decide to wait or stop, can you thank him, give a last offering and take down the shrine with respect? That is a good ending too."]},{kind:"dedication",title:"The Dedication",intro:"If you choose to go on, this is your self-dedication to Set: a promise you write and speak yourself. It is not an initiation into Kemetic Orthodoxy, the Temple of Set or any other group, and it does not replace one. An evening at sunset is fitting, when the sun boat enters the night; avoid his own unlucky birthday for this, and choose a calm night or the evening after a storm.",days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-Dedication to Set",summary:"A full evening rite in the Egyptian shape: purification, opening, adoration by his titles, offering, the vow, the spear raised against chaos, the reversion and closing.",minutes:45,ingredients:[{name:"Set",ref:"traditional/deity/set"},{name:"An offering bowl of fresh water",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"Natron, washing soda or salt in a cup of water, for washing"},{name:"Bread, and red wine, red beer or red juice",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"Frankincense or cedar and an incense burner",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense"},{name:"A red or black candle",ref:"traditional/candle/red"},{name:"A staff, stick or wooden rod to stand for his spear"},{name:"Red jasper or hematite to keep on the shrine",ref:"traditional/crystal/red_jasper",optional:!0},{name:"Your vow, written by hand"}],preparation:`During the day, write your vow. Keep it short, honest and keepable: what you will do (for example, evening water, his birthday kept, one act of strength or protection offered each week), what you will not do in his name (harm the innocent, curse, bully), for how long (a year and a day is common), and what you ask of him.

Before the rite, bathe, rinse your mouth with natron or salt water, and dress in clean clothes. Clean the shrine and set everything out, with his image covered. Lay the staff before the shrine.`,steps:[{title:"Purify",instructions:"Light the candle and incense. Wash your hands in the natron water, touch it to your lips, and pass your hands through the smoke.",light:!0,spoken:"I am pure, I am pure. I have washed, I have passed through the smoke. I come before the lord of the red land with clean hands."},{title:"Open the shrine",instructions:"Uncover his image. Stand straight.",spoken:"The doors are opened. Your face is uncovered, Sutekh, great of strength. Awake in peace."},{title:"Adore",instructions:"Raise both hands, palms toward him, and speak the praise slowly.",spoken:"Homage to you, Set, great of strength, lord of Nubt, lord of the red land and the storm, lord of the oasis, beloved of kings. You who stand in the prow of the boat of millions of years and spear the serpent of chaos, so that the sun may rise: I praise you."},{title:"Offer",instructions:"Lift the water, the bread and the red drink toward him and set them down.",spoken:"An offering for Set: water and bread, red drink and sweet smoke, every good and pure thing. May it please you."},{title:"Speak your vow",instructions:"Stand and read your written vow aloud, clearly. Sign and date it.",spoken:"This is my word, given freely and honestly. I will keep it as well as I am able. Hear me, Set."},{title:"Raise the spear",instructions:"Take up the staff and hold it before you, pointing away from you and toward the floor, as Set holds his spear down toward the serpent. Name, aloud, the chaos you will stand against: not a person, but a force, a habit, a danger.",minutes:3,spoken:"I stand in the prow with you. My strength is turned against chaos, never against the innocent. Let the boat pass safely."},{title:"Sit with him",instructions:"Lay down the staff and sit before him in silence. Notice what you feel in your body.",minutes:8},{title:"Reversion and closing",instructions:"Thank him. Eat a piece of the bread. Lay your vow at the foot of his image. Cover the shrine and snuff the candle. Step back before you turn away.",snuff:!0,spoken:"What you have received, I share. Go in peace, Set, great of strength. I am yours, and I will return."}],closing:"Pour the water and any drink on the earth outside the next morning. Keep the staff near his shrine. Record the rite in your journal, and reread your vow when you feel your anger or strength pulling the wrong way.",safety:"Keep flame and incense on heatproof surfaces in a ventilated room, away from children, pets and pet birds, and never leave them burning unattended. Handle the staff with care and keep it pointed down and away from people. Do not drink alcohol if you should not; red juice is a fine offering. Keep wine and grapes away from dogs."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"Strength at the Edges",intro:"After the dedication comes the long part: water each evening, strength offered in acts, his days kept, and a yearly renewal of your vow.",days:365,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"set-daily",title:"Evening water",instructions:'Most evenings, give fresh water, raise your hands, say "great of strength", and name one hard thing faced that day. On difficult days, the water and the title are enough.',times:365,every:"day"},{id:"set-weekly",title:"A weekly offering of strength",instructions:"Once a week (or once in every ten days, the Egyptian week), give bread and a red drink with incense, eat the bread afterwards, and offer him one act of effort, courage or protection from that week by name.",times:52,every:"week",prompt:"What strength did you offer this week, and where did it go?"},{id:"set-holy-days",title:"His holy days",instructions:`Keep his birthday on the third epagomenal day quietly, with a simple offering and no other ritual business, as the old calendars counted it unlucky. Greet him in great storms from a safe place indoors. If you keep the sun's journey, give a word at sunset now and then for the boat's safe passage through the night, remembering the god in its prow.

Use the calendar you have chosen, and keep it from year to year.`,times:2,every:"holy-day",prompt:"How did you keep the day, and what did it ask of you?"},{id:"set-renewal",title:"Yearly rededication",instructions:"On the anniversary of your dedication, read the year\u2019s journal, write a new vow, and repeat the dedication rite. Change the promise honestly. If you are ending the dedication, thank him, give a last offering and take down the shrine with respect.",times:1,prompt:"How has a year with Set changed the way you use your strength?"}]}],sources:["Herman te Velde, Seth, God of Confusion: A Study of His Role in Egyptian Mythology and Religion (Leiden, 1967; 2nd edn 1977)","The Contendings of Horus and Set (Papyrus Chester Beatty I) and the Memphite Theology (Shabaka Stone, British Museum EA 498), in Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature, vols. 1\u20132 (1973\u201376)",'Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris \xA7\xA712\u201313, 31, 49 and 62, trans. F. C. Babbitt (Loeb, 1936; public domain), LacusCurtius, on Set\u2019s birth, red animals, Typhon as the irrational, and iron as "the bone of Typhon"',"J. Gwyn Griffiths, The Conflict of Horus and Seth from Egyptian and Classical Sources (1960)","The Four Hundred Year Stela of Ramesses II (found at Tanis, now in Cairo); the Dakhla Stela (Ashmolean Museum); Olaf E. Kaper on the cult of Seth in Dakhleh Oasis (1997)","Papyrus Cairo 86637 (the Cairo Calendar of lucky and unlucky days)","Richard H. Wilkinson, The Complete Gods and Goddesses of Ancient Egypt (2003); Toby Wilkinson, Early Dynastic Egypt (1999); Erik Hornung, The Ancient Egyptian Books of the Afterlife (1999)"]};return f(y);})();
